---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Enterprise Sandbox: Your Special Projects Team on Demand | Word And Mouth" description="An AI-powered business simulator, the Vanguard Protocol, and a 100-Day Sprint methodology. Everything you need to scramble a Special Projects Team that actually ships.">
  <article class="case-study">
    <section class="case-header">
      <a href="/solved/" class="back-link">← Back to Solved</a>
      <div class="case-meta">
        <span class="problem-tag">Innovation Problem</span>
        <span class="case-date">January 2026</span>
        <span class="case-time">15 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Enterprise Sandbox</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">Large organisations are designed to protect the core. The corporate immune system attacks anything that looks risky. To build something bulletproof for the future, you must leave the building.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hero-quote">
      <blockquote>
        <p>"This is not R&D. This is a sanctioned insurrection."</p>
      </blockquote>
    </section>

    <section class="case-format">
      <div class="format-card problem-everyone-saw">
        <span class="format-number">1</span>
        <h2>The Problem Everyone Saw</h2>
        <p>"We need an innovation lab."</p>
        <p>Every large company has tried this. They set up an "Innovation Hub" with bean bags and whiteboards. They run hackathons. They hire a Chief Innovation Officer. They produce slide decks full of Post-it photos.</p>
        <p>Two years later, the lab is quietly defunded. Nothing shipped. The "Navy" (the main business) outmanoeuvred the "Pirates" (the innovators). The corporate antibodies won again.</p>
        <p>The cycle repeats every 3-5 years under a new name: Labs, Garage, Accelerator, Moonshot Factory. The result is always the same: ambitious idea → political strangulation → quiet burial.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card problem-actually-there">
        <span class="format-number">2</span>
        <h2>The Problem That Was Actually There</h2>
        <p><strong>The Innovator's Dilemma is structural, not cultural.</strong></p>
        
        <div class="insight-box">
          <h3>The Corporate Antibody Response</h3>
          <div class="delusion-grid">
            <div class="delusion-item">
              <h4>1. The ROI Trap</h4>
              <p>New ideas get measured by metrics designed for mature products. "What's the 3-year ROI?" But disruptive markets can't be measured with existing metrics. That's why they're disruptive.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="delusion-item">
              <h4>2. The Committee Problem</h4>
              <p>"Design by Committee" produces mediocrity. Every stakeholder adds requirements. The bold vision becomes an inoffensive nothing that disturbs no one and changes nothing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="delusion-item">
              <h4>3. The Proximity Problem</h4>
              <p>Culture is contagious. If the innovation team sits in HQ, they think like HQ. They attend the same meetings, follow the same processes, fear the same consequences.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="delusion-item">
              <h4>4. The Zombie Project</h4>
              <p>In a normal company, killing a project is career suicide. So teams drag out failing projects to protect their jobs. The walking dead consume budget while real opportunities starve.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card unexpected-solution">
        <span class="format-number">3</span>
        <h2>The Unexpected Solution</h2>
        <p><strong>The Vanguard Protocol: A complete operating system for Special Projects Teams.</strong></p>
        
        <p>We synthesised the methodologies of history's most successful innovation engines — Lockheed's Skunk Works, Google X, Amazon Lab126, DARPA, Disney Imagineering — into a unified playbook.</p>

        <div class="solution-showcase">
          <div class="strategy-grid">
            <div class="strategy-item">
              <h4>Kelly's 14 Rules</h4>
              <p>Absolute authority for the project lead. Viciously restricted headcount (10-25% of normal). Minimum paperwork. Trust vendors. Pay for performance, not headcount. The 1943 playbook still works.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="strategy-item">
              <h4>The Five Archetypes</h4>
              <p>You need the Pirate Captain (reality distorter), the MacGyver (constraint lover), the Zealot (true believer), the Whisperer (double agent), and the Assassin (the closer). Standard HR rejects them. You recruit them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="strategy-item">
              <h4>#MonkeyFirst</h4>
              <p>If the goal is to get a monkey to recite Shakespeare on a pedestal, don't build the pedestal first. That's easy. Tackle the hard part first. If the monkey can't be taught, kill the project before capital is wasted.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="strategy-item">
              <h4>The Corsair Compensation</h4>
              <p>Phantom stock tied to project value. Milestone bounties for speed. Repatriation clauses for safety. And the "Failure Bonus" — pay teams to kill their own bad projects early.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-section">
          <h4>The 100-Day Protocol</h4>
          <p>Not a roadmap. A survival guide.</p>
          <div class="protocol-phases">
            <div class="phase">
              <span class="phase-number">1</span>
              <div class="phase-content">
                <h5>Days 1-30: The Blackout</h5>
                <p>Move physically. Paper over the glass. Burn the PRD. Build the pretotype (fake landing page). If nobody clicks, kill now.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phase">
              <span class="phase-number">2</span>
              <div class="phase-content">
                <h5>Days 31-60: The Frankenstein</h5>
                <p>Junkyard sprint. Build for ONE user, not a million. The Mom Test — put it in strangers' hands. No explanations. First prototype sucks. Pivot.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phase">
              <span class="phase-number">3</span>
              <div class="phase-content">
                <h5>Days 61-90: The Street Fight</h5>
                <p>Real strangers, not friends. Kill Metric: did they pull out their credit card? Package the evidence.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="phase">
              <span class="phase-number">4</span>
              <div class="phase-content">
                <h5>Days 91-100: The Mic Drop</h5>
                <p>Don't start with slides. Put the product on the table. Let them touch it. "We have X users ready to pay Y dollars."</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="case-table">
          <h4>The 4 Causes of Death (Pre-Mortem)</h4>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Cause</th>
                <th>Antidote</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Organ Rejection (Sales won't sell it)</td>
                <td>Trojan Horse: solve their specific pain point</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Scope Creep (Ferrari engine, no car)</td>
                <td>Kill Metric Tattoo: does this impact the metric?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Ivory Tower Mutiny (Navy hates Pirates)</td>
                <td>Humble Pie: "We're janitors, not elite"</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Zombie Walk (lost urgency at Day 45)</td>
                <td>Burn the Boats: public Demo Day, invite investors</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="format-card what-you-can-steal">
        <span class="format-number">4</span>
        <h2>What You Can Steal</h2>
        
        <div class="steal-list">
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>1. The Air Gap</h4>
            <p>Physical separation isn't optional. If you can't rent a separate office, steal a conference room in the basement, paper over the glass, and change the locks. The corporate immune system cannot attack what it cannot find.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>2. The One-Key System</h4>
            <p>The project lead reports to ONE person: the CEO. No other executive has oversight, veto power, or "input." The CEO acts as the Shield, deflecting bureaucracy so the team can work.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>3. Metered Funding (Internal VC)</h4>
            <p>Not annual budget. Venture-style tranches. $50k for problem discovery. $250k for solution validation. $1M for technical feasibility. Hit the milestone, unlock the next tranche. Hit a kill signal, stop immediately.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>4. Bonus for Failure</h4>
            <p>If a team proves their project is unfeasible and recommends shutdown, they receive a "Smart Fail" bonus and immediate repatriation to a new project. It's cheaper to pay $50k to kill a bad idea in Month 3 than $2M to drag it out for two years.</p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="steal-item">
            <h4>5. The Pirate Psychology</h4>
            <p>"It's better to be a pirate than join the navy." Recruit founders (even failed ones), polymaths with non-linear careers, and constraint breakers who hack systems to get results. If they ask about promotion track in the first interview, don't hire them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-verdict">
      <h2>The Bigger Picture</h2>
      <p>This isn't just a methodology. It's the realisation that drives everything we do at Word And Mouth.</p>
      <p><strong>We ARE a Special Projects Team.</strong></p>
      <p>When you hire us, you're not hiring consultants who produce slide decks. You're scrambling a crack squadron that operates outside your corporate immune system. We have the external perspective your insiders can't have, the speed your committees won't allow, and the permission to fail that your employees don't possess.</p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>The Greenhouse is our vehicle for delivering Special Projects capability to clients who can't build it themselves. 90 days. One big problem. Ship or kill.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>The simulator, the curriculum, the archetypes, the 100-Day Protocol — these are the tools. But the real product is us: the Whisperer, the MacGyver, and the Pirate Captain your organisation desperately needs but can't formally employ.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="curriculum-preview">
      <h3>The Special Projects Curriculum</h3>
      <p>We train your Head of Special Projects using the same methodology.</p>
      <div class="curriculum-grid">
        <div class="curriculum-module">
          <span class="module-number">101</span>
          <h4>The Economic Necessity</h4>
          <p><em>The Innovator's Dilemma</em> — Why the main business will inevitably kill the new business if left together.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="curriculum-module">
          <span class="module-number">201</span>
          <h4>Structural Design</h4>
          <p><em>The Ambidextrous Organization</em> — Distinct units, integrated leadership.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="curriculum-module">
          <span class="module-number">301</span>
          <h4>Operational Tactics</h4>
          <p><em>Skunk Works</em> — Kelly's 14 Rules: Small teams, total autonomy.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="curriculum-module">
          <span class="module-number">401</span>
          <h4>Phase Management</h4>
          <p><em>Loonshots</em> — How to transfer innovation back without it dying.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="curriculum-module">
          <span class="module-number">501</span>
          <h4>Cultural Warfare</h4>
          <p><em>Steve Jobs Ch.14</em> — Building "Pirate" identity against corporate antibodies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-cta">
      <h3>Ready to scramble your Special Projects Team?</h3>
      <p>We help organisations build innovation capability that doesn't get crushed by BAU. Whether you want us to BE your Special Projects Team for 90 days, or train your people to run one, the first step is the same: find the real problem.</p>
      <a href="mailto:dave@wordandmouth.com?subject=Special Projects Capability" class="cta-button">Start the Conversation</a>
    </section>

    <section class="case-sources">
      <h4>The Reading List</h4>
      <ul class="source-links">
        <li><a href="https://github.com/davethackeray/enterprize/tree/main/business-simulator" target="_blank">GitHub: Enterprise Simulator</a> — Original prototype</li>
        <li><em>The Innovator's Dilemma</em> — Clayton Christensen</li>
        <li><em>Skunk Works</em> — Ben Rich & Leo Janos</li>
        <li><em>Loonshots</em> — Safi Bahcall</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.lockheedmartin.com/content/dam/lockheed-martin/aero/photo/skunkworks/kellys-14-rules.pdf" target="_blank">Kelly's 14 Rules</a> — Lockheed Martin</li>
        <li><a href="https://x.company/blog/posts/a-peek-inside-the-moonshot-factory-operating-manual/" target="_blank">Moonshot Factory Operating Manual</a> — Google X</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
